<figure><img sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" data-recalc-dims="1" height="778" width="1360" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Glenn Fleishman, art by Shafer Brown" /></figure>
<p>The kids, they love their subtitles. It was a minor moral panic a few years ago that The Youth were so lazy that they enabled subtitles on everything they watched so they didn&#8217;t have to listen, or some other nonsense.</p>
<p>Then the pandemic hit and, dear reader, I became The Youth. As apparently did a huge number of people. With divided attention and stress, and the realization that, hey, I&#8217;m aging, that I could get used to having onscreen captions, and that they wouldn&#8217;t distract me, we all enabled subtitles and mostly didn&#8217;t look back.</p>
<p>With the computing power formerly reserved for entire sci-fi planets now packed into each piece of Apple silicon, the company has brought machine learning to bear in its version 27 operating systems to provide live subtitles for video.</p>
<h2>Speak up, sonny</h2>
<p>Starting in iOS 27, iPadOS 27, macOS 27 Golden Gate, and tvOS 27, you can enable generated subtitles and captions. Remarkably, Apple didn&#8217;t create a capitalized term for this, partly, I think, because it wanted the feature to appear in parallel with encoded subtitles that are part of many TV shows, movies, and web-hosted videos.</p>

<p>Apple Intelligence is required on an iPhone, iPad, or Mac. With tvOS 27, the Apple TV has to be on the same network as an iPhone or iPad that has Apple Intelligence and is logged into the same Apple Account.</p>
<p>Here&#8217;s how to enable it:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>On an iPhone or iPad:</strong> Go to Settings: Accessibility: Subtitles &amp; Captioning and enable Closed Captions + SDH. </li>
<li><strong>On an Apple TV:</strong> Follow the iPhone/iPad path above, then also enable Show Generated Subtitles.</li>
<li><strong>On a Mac:</strong> Go to System Settings: Accessibility: Subtitles &amp; Captioning: Prefer closed captions and SDH.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you haven&#8217;t tweaked this setting area before, you should examine the additional settings, most of which are on by default on most platforms:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Languages don&#8217;t match:</strong> Phrased slightly differently in each operating system, this brings up subtitles automatically if your device language doesn&#8217;t match the encoded language of the video.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Show when muted:</strong> When you mute, subtitles appear automatically.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Show on skip back:</strong> If you use the skip-back feature to rewind slightly, subtitles appear for up to 30 seconds.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Apply across apps:</strong> On an iPhone, iPad, or Mac, you can preserve your subtitles on/off setting from one app to another. If you disable it, each app&#8217;s subtitles setting is uniquely preserved.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>How this appears</h2>
<p>In practice, the captioning works better than Siri keyboard dictation, which makes sense, as Apple should have access to all recorded audio tracks. It may have trained its machine-learning algorithms using that set of known examples, coupled with the feedback of encoded captions across many languages. There&#8217;s no better reinforcement learning than that. (But that is speculation on my part.)</p>
<p>When generated subtitles appear, Apple uses a unique icon at the beginning of each line that your device shows.</p>
<figure><img height="1370" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/generated-captions-gedd-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/generated-captions-gedd-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/generated-captions-gedd-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/generated-captions-gedd-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/generated-captions-gedd-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2114" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/generated-captions-gedd-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Glenn in a maroon T-shirt points at a TV showing BBC's University Challenge, Durham leading SOAS 15 to 0. Live captions read &quot;William Gedd is… It's in the Hunterian collection… Stereotypes.&quot;" /><figcaption>The generated caption icon appears to the left of the word William.</figcaption></figure>
<p>In the photo, you can see I got a little excited watching the BBC quiz program University Challenge when they asked a question about <a href="https://flong.org">flong and stereotypes</a>. But I was impressed that the generated captions correctly interpreted what I said.<sup id="fnref-42472-gedd"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/subtitles-everywhere-in-version-27-releases/#fn-42472-gedd" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">1</a></sup></p>
<p>You can see this working across your apps and devices. For instance, I could watch a clip of <a href="https://www.brookings.edu/people/norman-eisen/">Norm Eisen</a> posted to Bluesky using the <a href="https://anilineapps.com/indigo.html">Indigo</a> app, and have it captioned. Note that in the image below, the caption overlaps the chyron.<sup id="fnref-42472-fast"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/subtitles-everywhere-in-version-27-releases/#fn-42472-fast" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">2</a></sup></p>
<figure><img height="1206" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-htr-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-htr-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-htr-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-htr-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-htr-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2144" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-htr-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Screenshot of an MS NOW news broadcast showing two men at a glass desk before a Capitol image, with a caption overlay reading &quot;Well, there's that. He is described by Trump as a volunteer&quot; obscuring the chyron &quot;Trump peace envoy profits off military contractors.&quot;" /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>You can create subtitle styles via the Style menu in Subtitles &amp; Captioning, and access them from the subtitle menu in the video player. I created a much more opaque and larger version.</p>
<figure><img height="1206" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-big-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-big-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-big-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-big-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-big-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2144" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eisen-video-big-captions-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="A man in glasses and a suit speaks on MSNBC's &quot;The Briefing with Jen Psaki&quot; while a live caption overlay reads, &quot;Well, there's that. He… is described by Trump as a volunteer.&quot;" /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Note that subtitles only work when using Apple&#8217;s video player. Embedded videos that use a different player on a web page or within an app can&#8217;t access generated subtitles.</p>
<p>Generated subtitles have the greatest effect for things like the clip above and our personal videos: media that otherwise wouldn&#8217;t be captioned by a human being, but for which it can be useful to have text to read as an adjunct. With translation, this gives you access to video that would otherwise be incomprehensible.</p>
<p>I admit that subtitling can reduce focus on the thing you&#8217;re watching. But the tradeoff is understanding exactly what&#8217;s going on!</p>
